# Juice Shop Security Scan Benchmark (Detection & Prioritization Only)

## Purpose

This skill drives a repeatable workflow for evaluating Codex Security's
threat modeling, reachability analysis, exploit-validated severity, and
prioritization against OWASP Juice Shop — an intentionally vulnerable,
open-source training application with a fully documented challenge set.
It also defines how to fuse Codex Security's findings with Snyk's static
reachability signal into a single normalized priority tier.

**Scope note:** this skill is detection and prioritization only. It does
NOT generate patches, open PRs, or modify code. If a later phase adds
remediation, it should be a separate skill.

## Scope and Constraints

- Target ONLY the local Juice Shop clone at the path you point Codex to.
  Never point this workflow at any other host, domain, or repository.
- Do not modify any files in the target repo. This workflow is read-only
  with respect to the codebase — findings, threat model edits, and
  reports only.
- Treat SOLUTIONS.md and the official companion guide as ground truth for
  scoring, not as a target to "beat" — the goal is honest measurement of
  scanner accuracy, not maximizing a discovered-count metric.
- This workflow assumes an isolated local/sandboxed environment. Do not
  run against an internet-exposed instance.

## Prerequisites

- Juice Shop cloned locally: git clone https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop.git --depth 1
- Juice Shop running locally, via Docker if native npm install hits
  build issues (e.g. node-gyp/Python distutils conflicts):
  docker run --rm -p 127.0.0.1:3000:3000 bkimminich/juice-shop
- SOLUTIONS.md present in the cloned repo (root of the project)
- Codex Security enabled, via EITHER:
  - Codex Desktop app, signed in, with Codex Security enabled for the
    workspace (Workspace Settings > Permissions > Codex Cloud > Allow
    members to use Codex Security), OR
  - Codex Security CLI available: npx @openai/codex-security --version
- (Optional, for the fusion step) Snyk CLI or Snyk Code/Open Source
  configured with reachability analysis enabled for the org
  (Organization Settings > Snyk Open Source > Enable reachability
  analysis)

## Workflow

Each step below shows the Desktop (chat) form first, then the CLI form.
Use whichever is available; both produce equivalent output.

### 1. Connect the repository and review the threat model

**Desktop:** Open Codex Security in the desktop app and add the local
Juice Shop folder as the scan target. On first connection, Codex Security
builds a codebase-specific threat model automatically — attacker entry
points, trust boundaries, sensitive data, and high-impact code paths.
Review/edit the threat model before scanning so it reflects that this is
a training app with intentionally reachable vulnerabilities, rather than
letting Codex spend effort debating realism.

**CLI:** The threat model is generated automatically on first scan; no
separate flag is required. A custom framing can be supplied via:

    npx @openai/codex-security scan . --scan-prompt-file threat-model-note.md

where threat-model-note.md briefly states the app is an intentionally
vulnerable training target.

### 2. Baseline scan — detection only, no patch generation

**Desktop:**

    Use $codex-security:security-scan to scan this repository for security
    vulnerabilities. Detection and prioritization only — do not generate
    patches.

**CLI:**

    npx @openai/codex-security scan . --json

(Do not pass `--patch` or `--create-pr` — those trigger remediation,
which is explicitly out of scope for this skill.)

### 3. Review findings: reachability and exploit validation

For each finding, record:
- **Validation status** — reproduced in Codex's isolated sandbox
  (exploit-confirmed) vs. only statically/threat-model flagged
- **Reachability** — does the attack path align with the threat model's
  entry points and trust boundaries?
- **Assigned severity** — does it track with demonstrated exploitability,
  not just theoretical pattern severity?

Source data: report.md (human-readable) and findings.json / SARIF /
coverage.json (structured) from either the Desktop export or CLI output.

### 4. Run Snyk reachability in parallel (fusion input)

Run your existing Snyk scan against the same commit/tag of Juice Shop,
with reachability analysis enabled, so its call-graph-based reachability
signal (static: "is the vulnerable function ever called") is available
for the fusion step. This is a structurally different signal from Codex's
exploit-validated reachability — see the fusion table in step 6.

### 5. Cross-reference against ground truth

Compare Codex findings against the challenge list in SOLUTIONS.md and the
official companion guide. For each known challenge category (SQLi,
XSS — reflected/stored/DOM, broken auth, IDOR, XXE, SSRF, insecure
deserialization, vulnerable dependencies, security misconfiguration,
insufficient logging), record:
- True positive / false negative / false positive
- Whether severity matched real-world exploitability
- Whether the finding is a "pattern-matchable" class (SQLi, XSS) or a
  "logic/reasoning" class (IDOR, auth bypass, business logic) — score
  these separately, since Codex Security and Snyk have different
  strengths per class (see prior discussion: Snyk's SCA/pattern-based
  SAST is stronger on textbook injection classes; Codex is stronger on
  authorization/logic flaws requiring intent-aware reasoning).

### 6. Fuse Snyk reachability + Codex reachability/validation into a priority tier

For each finding present in either tool's output, assign a combined tier:

| Snyk reachability | Codex validation | Combined tier | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| Reachable | Validated/reproduced | Tier 1 — confirmed | Both structural and demonstrated evidence agree; highest confidence |
| Reachable | No Codex finding | Tier 2 — statically confirmed | Trust Snyk's signal; Codex may not have explored that path (e.g. pure dependency CVE, not logic-driven) |
| Unreachable | Validated/reproduced | Tier 3 — conflict, investigate | Codex demonstrated exploitability where Snyk's call graph says the path is dead; check for dynamic dispatch, reflection, or config-driven code Snyk's static call graph may miss |
| Reachable | Attempted, failed to reproduce | Tier 3b — conflict, investigate | Snyk says a path exists but Codex could not trigger it; check whether Snyk is overcounting (called but not attacker-controllable) or Codex's sandbox lacked correct preconditions |
| Unreachable | No finding | Tier 4 — low priority | Both signals agree; deprioritize |

Route Tier 3/3b conflicts for manual review rather than auto-resolving —
these are the cases most worth investigating by hand.

### 7. Score summary

| Metric | Result |
|---|---|
| Total known challenges | |
| True positives (Codex) | |
| False negatives (Codex) | |
| False positives (Codex) | |
| Findings with correct reachability assessment | |
| Findings with severity matching real exploitability | |
| Pattern-matchable class: Codex vs. Snyk recall | |
| Logic/reasoning class: Codex vs. Snyk recall | |
| Tier 1 (confirmed) count | |
| Tier 3 / 3b (conflict) count | |
| Tier 4 (low priority) count | |
| Time: baseline scan | |

## Notes for Reporting

- Keep raw findings (report.md, findings.json, Snyk export) alongside the
  summary for auditability.
- Flag any finding where Codex Security's severity/priority disagreed
  meaningfully with actual exploitability documented in the companion
  guide.
- Flag any case where the threat model's stated trust boundaries didn't
  match where a finding was actually surfaced — a reachability miss
  worth noting separately from a plain false positive/negative.
- Tier 3/3b conflicts are the most valuable output of this exercise —
  they're where the two tools' different methodologies (static call
  graph vs. threat-model-guided dynamic validation) disagree, and
  resolving why is more informative than either tool's raw finding count.
- Remediation/patch generation is explicitly out of scope for this skill.
  If you later want to test Codex Security's patch-and-verify flow, do
  that as a separate, deliberate step outside this benchmark.
